from tnx import *
def E(u,un,*a): EN(9,u,un,*a)
U1="Unit 1: Learning the Game, Stopping by Woods, The Envious Neighbour"
E(1,U1,"learning-the-game","Learning the Game (Sachin Tendulkar)",
 "Eleven-year-old Sachin walked into a cricket camp full of strangers and had to prove he belonged there.",
 "In this autobiographical prose piece, Sachin Tendulkar remembers how he began serious cricket. His brother Ajit took him, aged eleven, to Ramakant Achrekar Sir's summer camp at Shivaji Park. Nervous under so many watching eyes, Sachin batted badly and the coach thought he was too young. Ajit persuaded Sir to give him another chance; this time Sachin played more freely and was accepted.",
 "The lesson shows that talent needs hard daily practice, a patient guide and a supportive family. The schedule was tough: long morning and evening sessions, an early bus, and only one set of cricket clothes that had to be washed and dried between sessions. Sachin never complained, because he loved the game.",
 "As you read, note words about routine and effort such as camp, rigorous, nets, schedule and exhausted. The text is written in the first person and the past tense, which is typical of an autobiography.",
 ["autobiography","camp","rigorous","nets","schedule"],
 ("Sachin was selected at the camp on his very first try.",["He did badly at first.","His brother Ajit asked for a second chance."]),
 ["m|Who took Sachin to the cricket camp?|His brother Ajit|His father|His friend|Ajit took him to Achrekar Sir.","m|Where was the camp held?|Shivaji Park|Marina Beach|Eden Gardens|The camp was at Shivaji Park.","t|Sachin batted very well at his first trial.|F|He felt overawed and did not make an impact.","m|What does 'rigorous' mean?|very strict and demanding|very easy|very short|A rigorous schedule is tough.","s|Who was Sachin's coach?|Achrekar;Ramakant Achrekar|Achrekar Sir was the coach.","t|The text is an autobiography.|T|It is written by Sachin about his own life."])
E(1,U1,"stopping-by-woods","Stopping by Woods on a Snowy Evening (Robert Frost)",
 "Imagine stopping your cycle on a quiet road just to watch snow fall on a forest at night.",
 "A traveller driving a horse-drawn sleigh pauses beside a forest on the darkest evening of the year to watch snow fall. The woods belong to someone who lives in the village and will not see him stop. His little horse finds the halt strange, because there is no farmhouse near, and shakes its harness bells as if to ask a question.",
 "The woods are lovely, dark and deep, yet the speaker remembers his promises and the long road ahead. The repeated last line, miles to go before I sleep, can be read as duty to finish daily tasks and, for some readers, as the journey of life. The poem balances the pull of rest and beauty against responsibility.",
 "Frost uses simple words, a regular rhyme pattern (each stanza's third line rhymes with the next stanza) and repetition for effect. Notice images such as downy flake, frozen lake and easy wind.",
 ["stanza","rhyme","repetition","imagery","downy"],
 ("The traveller decides to stay in the woods for the night.",["He admires them but has promises to keep.","He moves on towards home."]),
 ["m|Who wrote this poem?|Robert Frost|William Blake|Ruskin Bond|Robert Frost, an American poet.","m|What made the horse think it was queer?|Stopping with no farmhouse near|The bells|The snow|There was no house nearby.","t|The speaker stays in the woods.|F|He has promises to keep and goes on.","m|Which line is repeated at the end?|And miles to go before I sleep|The woods are lovely|Whose woods these are|It stresses duty and distance.","s|Which season is suggested by the snow?|winter|Snow falls in winter.","t|The poem has a regular rhyme scheme.|T|Yes, the stanzas are linked by rhyme."])
E(1,U1,"envious-neighbour","The Envious Neighbour (Japanese folk tale)",
 "What happens to people who watch their neighbour's good luck and want it for themselves?",
 "An honest old couple had a pet dog that began to bark and wag its tail in the garden. They dug there and found gold, silver and precious things, shared some with the poor and became comfortably wealthy. Their stingy, covetous neighbours borrowed the dog hoping for the same luck and were disappointed.",
 "A folk tale passes on a lesson through a simple story. Here the lesson is that kindness and honesty are rewarded, while envy and greed lead to loss. The characters are clearly good or bad, as in most folk tales.",
 "Useful words: envious, covetous, stingy, treasure and alms. Notice how the opening phrase In the old, old days sets the story in a timeless past.",
 ["folk tale","envious","covetous","stingy","alms"],
 ("The neighbours became rich because they were clever.",["They were greedy and stingy.","Honest people were rewarded in the story."]),
 ["m|The couple's pet was a|dog|cat|parrot|A dog found the treasure.","t|The honest couple gave alms to the poor.|T|They gave alms after finding the treasure.","m|'Covetous' means|greedily wanting what others have|generous|sleepy|It describes the neighbours.","m|What is the main lesson?|Honesty is rewarded and envy brings loss|Pets are lucky|Gold is everywhere|Good and bad conduct bring results.","s|What country does the tale come from?|Japan|It is a Japanese folk tale.","t|The neighbours were generous people.|F|They were stingy and covetous."])
U2="Unit 2: I Can't Climb Trees Anymore, A Poison Tree, The Fun They Had"
E(2,U2,"cant-climb-trees","I Can't Climb Trees Anymore (Ruskin Bond)",
 "A grown man stands at a garden wall looking at the house where he spent his boyhood.",
 "A visitor comes back to the old granite house of his childhood and sees the jackfruit tree still standing. A girl of twelve or thirteen speaks to him from the other side of the wall. As they talk, he remembers his grandmother's saying that a blessing rests on a house where a tree's shadow falls, and thinks of the treasures he once left there.",
 "The story is about memory, the passing of time and the friendship that can grow between a young girl and an older man. The title tells us that the man who once climbed trees easily now finds that his age stops him, though his love for the tree has not changed.",
 "Learn the shortened forms in the exercise (I'd, he's, wasn't, won't) and the vocabulary words drenched, dizzy, flourishing and prosperous. The writer paints a peaceful scene with careful details such as hollyhocks, the turnstile and the jackfruit tree.",
 ["visitor","jackfruit tree","turnstile","blessing","nostalgia"],
 ("The visitor still climbs trees as easily as before.",["The title says he can't.","He is older now."]),
 ["m|Who wrote this story?|Ruskin Bond|Sachin Tendulkar|Robert Frost|Ruskin Bond wrote it.","m|Which tree still stood by the house?|Jackfruit tree|Mango tree|Neem tree|The jackfruit tree.","t|A girl spoke to the visitor from behind the wall.|T|She stood between the hollyhocks.","m|What saying did the visitor's grandmother repeat?|A blessing rests where a tree's shadow falls|Early to bed|Look before you leap|It is about the tree's blessing.","s|What was in the opening where a turnstile once stood?|wall;a wall|The opening was walled up.","t|The house had changed completely.|F|It had not changed much."])
E(2,U2,"a-poison-tree","A Poison Tree (William Blake)",
 "What happens to anger that you hide and feed instead of talking about it?",
 "The speaker tells his friend that he is angry and the anger ends. He does not tell his foe, and the wrath grows. He waters it with fears and sunshines it with smiles and soft deceitful wiles until it bears a bright apple. The foe sees the apple, steals into the garden at night and is found lying dead under the tree in the morning.",
 "The tree is a metaphor for hidden anger: unspoken hatred grows and becomes poisonous. The poem warns that bottled-up resentment harms others and ourselves, and that open, honest talk ends quarrels.",
 "Blake writes in short, rhyming couplets with a simple, almost song-like rhythm. Terms to know: wrath, foe, wiles, deceitful and metaphor.",
 ["metaphor","wrath","foe","wiles","couplet"],
 ("Hidden anger disappears by itself.",["It grew in the poem.","Telling the friend ended the anger."]),
 ["m|Who wrote A Poison Tree?|William Blake|Robert Frost|Aaron Shepard|William Blake.","m|What does the poison tree stand for?|Hidden anger|A real tree|The sun|It is a metaphor.","t|The speaker told his friend about his anger.|T|And the anger ended.","m|What did the tree bear?|An apple|A rose|A mango|A bright apple.","s|What does 'foe' mean?|enemy|An enemy.","t|The poem advises us to hide our anger.|F|It warns against doing so."])
E(2,U2,"fun-they-had","The Fun They Had (Isaac Asimov)",
 "Would you rather learn from a teacher in a classroom or from a screen that adjusts to you?",
 "In the year 2157, eleven-year-old Margie and thirteen-year-old Tommy find an old printed book in the attic. Margie is taught at home by a mechanical teacher, a screen with a slot for homework, and she dislikes it. She reads about children who once went to a school with a human teacher and learned together, and she imagines what fun they had.",
 "The story is science fiction. It asks us to think about technology in learning and about what a machine cannot give, such as friends, shared laughter and a caring human teacher. It also shows that old things like books can seem wonderful to a later generation.",
 "Words to note: mechanical, attic, telebook, geared and typical. The setting in the future uses the present tense of the narrator's world with a past-tense plot.",
 ["science fiction","mechanical teacher","attic","telebook","year 2157"],
 ("Margie loved her mechanical teacher.",["She disliked it.","She imagined the fun of old schools."]),
 ["m|Who wrote this story?|Isaac Asimov|Ruskin Bond|Mark Twain|Isaac Asimov.","m|What did the children find?|An old printed book|A robot|A map|They found a real book.","t|Margie enjoyed her mechanical teacher.|F|She hated school at home.","m|In which year is the story set?|2157|1957|2057|It is set in 2157.","s|How old was Margie?|eleven;11|Margie was eleven.","t|The story is science fiction.|T|It imagines future schooling."])
E(3,"Unit 3: Old Man River, On Killing a Tree, Earthquake","old-man-river","Old Man River (drama by Dorothy Deming)",
 "When a river rises beyond its banks, what matters most is courage, cooperation and being prepared.",
 "Old Man River is a short play to be read aloud in scenes, with stage directions in brackets. Its characters include the teenage Marshall sisters, Amy and Betty, and their young neighbour Rose Field. The setting is a flood, and the play shows how people respond when rising water threatens their homes.",
 "The unit links natural disasters such as cyclones, floods, earthquakes and tsunamis with human activities that harm nature, and asks us to think about precautions. A play teaches through dialogue, so pay attention to how each character speaks and acts under pressure.",
 "Learn drama terms: scene, stage direction, dialogue, cast and enact. When you read aloud, follow the brackets for actions and tone.",
 ["drama","scene","stage direction","dialogue","flood"],
 ("A play is read only silently.",["Plays are acted or read aloud.","Stage directions guide the actors."]),
 ["m|What is Old Man River?|A drama|A poem|A novel|It is a play in scenes.","m|Which natural disaster is the setting?|A flood|A cyclone|A drought|It is about a flood.","t|Stage directions are written in brackets.|T|They guide the actors.","m|Who are Amy and Betty?|Sisters|Neighbours|Teachers|Amy Marshall and her younger sister.","s|What are the speaking parts in a play called?|dialogue|Dialogue.","t|The drama has no characters.|F|It has several characters."],
 "(The lesson is based on the textbook's introduction to the play and its warm-up tasks; read the full drama in your book.)")
E(3,"Unit 3: Old Man River, On Killing a Tree, Earthquake","on-killing-a-tree","On Killing a Tree (Gieve Patel)",
 "A tree stands for years with leaves, shade and fruit; how hard is it to remove it?",
 "The poet explains that killing a tree is not easy. A simple jab of the knife does not do it. The tree has grown slowly, drinking in sun, air and soil, so it has to be hacked, pulled out by the roots, scorched and left to die slowly in the sun.",
 "The poem is a quiet protest against cutting down trees. By describing how long a tree takes to grow and how much effort it takes to destroy it, the poet makes us respect nature. Only when the roots are exposed and the green fades does the tree finally die.",
 "Notice the many verbs of violence: hack, chop, pull, scorch. Watch for the contrast between the slow growth and quick destruction, and the use of everyday words.",
 ["roots","scorch","hack","protest","nature"],
 ("Killing a tree is quick and easy.",["The poem says it takes effort.","The tree grew slowly."]),
 ["m|Who wrote On Killing a Tree?|Gieve Patel|Blake|Frost|Gieve Patel.","m|What does the poem show about trees?|They are strong and take long to grow|They are weak|They are useless|It stresses the tree's strength.","t|A jab of the knife is enough to kill a tree.|F|It takes much more.","m|What must be pulled out to kill the tree?|The roots|The fruit|The leaves|Roots are exposed.","s|Name one thing that gives a tree life besides soil.|sun;air;sunshine;water|Sun, air and water feed it.","t|The poem respects nature.|T|It values the tree."])
E(3,"Unit 3: Old Man River, On Killing a Tree, Earthquake","earthquake","Earthquake (M. S. Mahadevan)",
 "A teashop owner at a Garhwal bus stop tells a stranger his own story over a hot cup of tea.",
 "The narrator, Brij, is a seventeen-year-old who runs a small tea shop on the road to Badrinath in Garhwal. He greets a tired traveller and invites him to warm up at the shop while he makes tea. As he talks, he tells about his work and about an earthquake that changed the place and his life.",
 "The story is told in the first person, in a friendly, direct way as if Brij speaks to you. It reminds us to help others, especially in hard times, and shows how young people can be responsible and brave. The warm-up question asks when you last helped someone.",
 "Look at the conversational words such as Greetings, traveller, Sir and My name. Learn nouns like pilgrim, valley and teashop.",
 ["Garhwal","Badrinath","teashop","pilgrim","first-person narrator"],
 ("Brij is an old man who works in a big city.",["He is seventeen.","He runs a teashop in Garhwal."]),
 ["m|Who is the narrator?|Brij|Ruskin Bond|Asimov|Brij tells the story.","m|How old is Brij?|Seventeen|Twelve|Thirty|He is seventeen.","t|Brij runs a tea shop.|T|On the road to Badrinath.","m|Where does the story take place?|Garhwal|Chennai|Madurai|Near Badrinath, Garhwal.","s|What does Brij offer the traveller?|tea;a cup of tea|He offers tea.","t|The story is in the third person.|F|Brij speaks as I."],
 "(Written from the opening of the story in the textbook; read the full text in your book for the events of the earthquake.)")
E(4,"Unit 4: Seventeen Oranges, The Spider and the Fly, The Cat and the Painkiller","seventeen-oranges","Seventeen Oranges (Bill Naughton)",
 "A young cart driver at the docks loved oranges so much that one day they got him into trouble.",
 "The narrator drove a pony-and-cart for the Swift Delivery Company and often went in and out of the docks. He was not a real thief, but he often picked up fruit that fell from the boats and hid it under a big apron. Clem Jones planned thefts carefully, but the narrator took things only when he found them, until a policeman found seventeen oranges hidden in his pockets.",
 "The story is humorous but it carries a clear moral: even small dishonest acts can have big consequences. It also shows the difference between a careful planner and an opportunist, and how the narrator never ate an orange again after being locked up.",
 "Note the colloquial, first-person style and words like docks, apron, pony-and-cart, theft and planner.",
 ["docks","apron","pony-and-cart","theft","narrator"],
 ("The narrator carefully planned his thefts.",["He took things when he found them.","Clem Jones was the planner."]),
 ["m|Who wrote Seventeen Oranges?|Bill Naughton|Mark Twain|Isaac Asimov|Bill Naughton.","m|Where did the narrator work?|At the docks with a pony-and-cart|In a school|On a farm|He drove for Swift Delivery.","t|The narrator hid fruit under an apron.|T|He made a big apron.","m|Who was a careful planner?|Clem Jones|The narrator|The policeman|Clem planned carefully.","s|How many oranges were found in his pockets?|seventeen;17|Seventeen.","t|He loved bananas more than oranges.|F|He liked oranges best."])
E(4,"Unit 4: Seventeen Oranges, The Spider and the Fly, The Cat and the Painkiller","spider-and-the-fly","The Spider and the Fly (Mary Howitt)",
 "Sweet words from a stranger can hide a trap.",
 "A spider invites a fly into his parlour with flattering words. The fly refuses again and again, because she knows that those who go in never come out. At last the spider praises her wings and eyes so much that she is tempted, goes closer and is caught in his web.",
 "The poem is a cautionary tale about flattery. The moral is to beware of people who praise us too much to get what they want. It ends with a warning to young people not to trust flattering words.",
 "Words to learn: parlour, flattery, cunning, winding stair and web. The poem uses a story-like rhyme and a repeated invitation, Will you walk into my parlour.",
 ["flattery","parlour","cunning","moral","web"],
 ("The fly was never tempted.",["She was finally tempted.","Flattery made her go near."]),
 ["m|Who wrote The Spider and the Fly?|Mary Howitt|Blake|Frost|Mary Howitt.","m|What does the spider use to trap the fly?|Flattery|Force|Food|He praised her.","t|The fly entered the web.|T|She was caught.","m|What is the moral?|Beware of flattery|Spiders are kind|Flies are brave|Do not trust flatterers.","s|What is a trap made of threads called?|web;a web|A spider's web.","t|The spider is honest.|F|He is cunning."])
E(4,"Unit 4: Seventeen Oranges, The Spider and the Fly, The Cat and the Painkiller","cat-and-the-painkiller","The Cat and the Painkiller (Mark Twain, from Tom Sawyer)",
 "Tom Sawyer hates his medicine so much that he finds a very odd way to get rid of it.",
 "Aunt Polly believes in new health cures and forces Tom to take a bitter medicine called Painkiller. Tom pretends he likes it and asks for more. When she is not looking, he gives a spoonful to the cat Peter, who leaps wildly around the room and knocks things over. Aunt Polly finally sees what has happened.",
 "The extract shows Tom's mischief and quick thinking and Aunt Polly's caring but fussy ways. It is humorous, and the writer gives characters lively dialogue. The novel The Adventures of Tom Sawyer is set along the Mississippi River in the town of St. Petersburg.",
 "Words to learn: painkiller, mischief, dose, pet and Aunt. Notice how humour comes from a wild cat and a boy's clever excuses.",
 ["Tom Sawyer","Aunt Polly","painkiller","mischief","extract"],
 ("Tom really liked the Painkiller.",["He pretended to.","He gave it to the cat."]),
 ["m|Who wrote Tom Sawyer?|Mark Twain|Bill Naughton|Dorothy Deming|Mark Twain.","m|Who lived with Tom?|Aunt Polly|His mother|His teacher|Aunt Polly and his brother Sid.","t|Tom gave the medicine to the cat.|T|Peter the cat took it.","m|What was the cat's name?|Peter|Sid|Tom|The cat was Peter.","s|What was the medicine called?|Painkiller|It was called Painkiller.","t|The cat stayed calm after the dose.|F|It went wild."])
E(5,"Unit 5: Water - The Elixir of Life, The River, Little Cyclone","water-elixir-of-life","Water - The Elixir of Life (Sir C. V. Raman)",
 "People once searched for a magic drink that gave endless life, but the real elixir was always near.",
 "Sir C. V. Raman says that people have long dreamed of an elixir of life, the divine Amrita, which would give immortality. He argues that the true elixir is plain water. He describes the line where the Libyan Desert meets the fertile Nile valley: on one side lifeless sand, on the other green fields and crowded towns, and the difference is water.",
 "The essay also stresses conserving water. Soil erosion lets rain water run away quickly, so measures such as contour cultivation and planting suitable vegetation keep water on and in the soil. Such measures help both soil and water, so they serve a double purpose.",
 "Words to learn: elixir, immortality, erosion, vegetation, contour cultivation and conserve. The essay uses examples and a personal memory to make a scientific point.",
 ["elixir","erosion","contour cultivation","vegetation","conserve"],
 ("Water is useful only for drinking.",["It supports all life and farming.","The Nile valley shows its value."]),
 ["m|Who wrote this essay?|Sir C. V. Raman|Ruskin Bond|Frost|Sir C. V. Raman.","m|What is the true elixir of life?|Plain water|Gold|Fire|Water.","t|The Nile valley is fertile because of its water.|T|The river makes it green.","m|What is contour cultivation?|Farming along slopes to check water flow|Cutting trees|Burning fields|It prevents soil erosion.","s|Which desert is next to the Nile valley in the essay?|Libyan;Libyan Desert|The Libyan Desert.","t|Soil erosion helps conserve water.|F|Preventing erosion helps conserve it."])
E(5,"Unit 5: Water - The Elixir of Life, The River, Little Cyclone","the-river","The River (Caroline Ann Bowles)",
 "A river changes as it travels from a sparkling stream to the wide sea.",
 "The poet addresses the river directly. At first it is little and bright, dancing over yellow pebbles like a child at play. Then it swells and rushes on like impetuous youth. Later it is broad, deep and still as time, and at last it dashes headlong into the sea, which the poem compares to eternity.",
 "The river's journey is compared to human life: childhood, youth, maturity and the end. The poem uses personification and similes to make the river seem alive.",
 "Notice the repeated address River, river and the similes beginning with like. Learn the words foliage, impetuous, brimming and eternity.",
 ["simile","personification","impetuous","eternity","foliage"],
 ("The poem is only about water.",["It compares the river to life.","The sea stands for eternity."]),
 ["m|Who wrote The River?|Caroline Ann Bowles|Blake|Gieve Patel|Caroline Ann Bowles.","m|The little river is compared to|A child at play|An old man|A mountain|Like a child at play.","t|The sea is compared to eternity.|T|It has no limit.","m|What does the broad, still river suggest?|Mature age|Childhood|Anger|Mortal prime.","s|Which figure of speech compares using 'like'?|simile|A simile.","t|The poem addresses the river directly.|T|It says River, river."])
E(5,"Unit 5: Water - The Elixir of Life, The River, Little Cyclone","little-cyclone","Little Cyclone: The Story of a Grizzly Cub (Ernest Thompson Seton)",
 "A young bear loses his mother and must face a strange new world alone.",
 "Little Cyclone was a grizzly cub who earned his name from his wild, stormy ways. When his mother was shot, he and his brother became orphans. One of them escaped, but Cyclone was captured and sent to the Zoological Park in New York. Most newcomers are scared on their first day, but when the box was opened he stood up with courage.",
 "The story shows courage and the bond between animals and their keepers. It also reminds us that wild animals suffer when their homes and families are disturbed by humans.",
 "Practise using phrases from the story in sentences. Learn the words grizzly, cub, orphan, captor and courageously.",
 ["grizzly","cub","orphan","captor","courageously"],
 ("Little Cyclone was afraid on his first day at the zoo.",["He stood up with courage.","Most newcomers are scared."]),
 ["m|Little Cyclone was a|grizzly cub|tiger cub|lion cub|A grizzly bear cub.","m|Where was he taken?|Zoological Park in New York|Chennai Zoo|A circus|He was taken to New York.","t|His mother was shot.|T|The cubs became orphans.","m|What does 'orphan' mean?|A child without parents|A pet|A hunter|Without parents.","s|Who wrote the story?|Seton;Ernest Thompson Seton|Ernest Thompson Seton.","t|Cyclone met every challenge bravely.|T|He was courageous."])
E(6,"Unit 6: From Zero to Infinity, The Comet, Mother's Voice","zero-to-infinity","From Zero to Infinity (Srinivasa Ramanujan)",
 "A young man from a small Tamil Nadu town filled notebooks with mathematical results that amazed Cambridge.",
 "The lesson is a biography of the mathematician Srinivasa Ramanujan. Working with G. H. Hardy at Cambridge, he made discoveries in number theory, partitions and continued fractions. He was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society on 28 February 1918 as the youngest Indian to receive the honour, and in October that year became the first Indian elected a Fellow of Trinity College.",
 "Ramanujan's story shows how passion, hard work and the right opportunity can lift a gifted person from humble beginnings. His birthday, 22 December, is celebrated in India as National Mathematics Day.",
 "Learn biography words: fellow, elected, distinguished, achievement, identities and circle method. Note the use of dates and the past tense.",
 ["biography","fellow","number theory","achievement","partition"],
 ("Ramanujan worked alone and never met other mathematicians.",["He worked with Hardy.","He went to Cambridge."]),
 ["m|Who worked with Ramanujan at Cambridge?|G. H. Hardy|Raman|Nehru|Hardy.","m|Which honour did he get in 1918?|Fellow of the Royal Society|Nobel Prize|Padma Shri|FRS.","t|He was the youngest Indian elected FRS.|T|That is stated in the text.","m|National Mathematics Day is on|22 December|14 November|2 October|His birthday.","s|He was the first Indian elected Fellow of which college?|Trinity;Trinity College|Trinity College, Cambridge.","t|Ramanujan's work covered number theory.|T|Yes."])
E(6,"Unit 6: From Zero to Infinity, The Comet, Mother's Voice","the-comet","The Comet (Norman Littleford)",
 "A comet streaks across the sky with a tail miles long.",
 "The poem describes a comet rampaging through the heavens day and night. It is faster than a cheetah, with a tail miles long, and bigger than a mountain. As it nears the Sun its outer ice melts, forming the bright tail.",
 "The poem links science and poetry: comets are bodies of ice and dust that orbit the Sun. The poet uses comparisons to show their speed and size and creates a sense of wonder about the universe.",
 "The warm-up crossword revises the solar system: Moon, Venus, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune, Mars, Jupiter, Mercury, Earth and the Sun. Learn light-year and spectacle.",
 ["comet","orbit","spectacle","solar system","light-year"],
 ("A comet is smaller than a cheetah.",["The poem says it is bigger than a mountain.","It is faster than a cheetah."]),
 ["m|Who wrote The Comet?|Norman Littleford|Frost|Blake|Norman Littleford.","m|Which is the Red Planet?|Mars|Venus|Jupiter|Mars is red.","t|The comet's outer ice melts near the Sun.|T|That makes the tail.","m|The comet is faster than a|cheetah|snail|tortoise|The poem says faster than a cheetah.","s|What is Earth's natural satellite?|Moon;the Moon|The Moon.","t|The comet's tail is a few metres long.|F|It is miles long."])
E(6,"Unit 6: From Zero to Infinity, The Comet, Mother's Voice","mothers-voice","Mother's Voice (Vasil Berezhnoy)",
 "Far from Earth, a man replays a recording to hear his mother's voice again.",
 "The story is set in the future on the Moon, in the tunnels of Selenopolis. The son walks with his mother, who is visiting for the first time, and shows her the structures that prop up the star-studded sky. Her ringing voice reflects her love of singing. At the end he plays back a recording and feels as if he is again walking with her on the Moon.",
 "The story is about love between mother and son and about how humanity, like the young, goes out to explore. It ends with the thought that humanity is young.",
 "Learn the words galaxy, chore, orchard, expedition and recording. Notice how a science-fiction setting carries a human emotion.",
 ["science fiction","Selenopolis","recording","galaxy","expedition"],
 ("The story is set only on Earth.",["It is set on the Moon.","The son is far from Earth."]),
 ["m|Where is the story set?|On the Moon|In Chennai|Under the sea|Selenopolis on the Moon.","m|Who wrote Mother's Voice?|Vasil Berezhnoy|Asimov|Twain|Vasil Berezhnoy.","t|The mother had a ringing voice.|T|She loved singing.","m|What does the son play back?|A recording|A song book|A film|He plays a recording.","s|What is the name of the Moon city?|Selenopolis|Selenopolis.","t|The story is about love and exploration.|T|Yes."])
E(7,"Unit 7: A Birthday Letter, The Stick-together Families, The Christmas Truce","a-birthday-letter","A Birthday Letter (Jawaharlal Nehru)",
 "Instead of a gift, a father sends his daughter a letter full of thoughts.",
 "Jawaharlal Nehru, India's first Prime Minister, wrote letters to his young daughter Indira while he was in prison. In this birthday letter he says that real gifts are not just things bought with money but good thoughts and wishes. He encourages her to be brave and good and to serve India.",
 "The letter shows his warmth as a father and teacher. It teaches that courage and goodness matter more than possessions.",
 "Learn letter-writing features: address, date, greeting, body and closing. Words to note: tutelage, architect, sovereign and eminent.",
 ["letter","tutelage","eminent","greeting","gift"],
 ("A real gift must always be bought.",["The letter gives thoughts.","Goodness matters more."]),
 ["m|To whom did Nehru write?|His daughter Indira|His friend|A teacher|It is a letter to his daughter.","m|Nehru was India's first|Prime Minister|President|Chief Justice|First PM.","t|The letter stresses courage.|T|He wants her to be brave.","m|Children called him|Uncle Nehru|Mr Gandhi|Sir|Uncle Nehru.","s|When was Nehru born (month)?|November|14 November 1889.","t|The letter is written in the third person.|F|It is written as I to you."])
E(7,"Unit 7: A Birthday Letter, The Stick-together Families, The Christmas Truce","stick-together-families","The Stick-together Families (Edgar A. Guest)",
 "Why do families who share their fun and their troubles seem to be the happiest?",
 "The poet praises families that stick together. Rich or poor, they find the finest mirth and hear the sweetest music because they share one another's joys and troubles. Brothers and sisters who go separate ways may shatter the charm of life.",
 "The poem celebrates unity and the joint family. Its central idea is that love and sharing, not money, bring the real joys of earth.",
 "Use the summary words brothers and sisters, joint family, bitterness and share their fun. Note the rhyming couplets and the cheerful tone.",
 ["joint family","unity","mirth","couplet","bitterness"],
 ("Only rich families are happy.",["Sticking together brings joy.","Rich or poor can be happy."]),
 ["m|Who wrote the poem?|Edgar A. Guest|Blake|Frost|Edgar Guest.","m|Who finds the sweetest music?|The stick-together family|The rich|The lonely|The stick-together family.","t|The poem praises unity.|T|Yes.","m|What can shatter the charm of life?|Going separate ways|Sharing|Laughter|Separation hurts.","s|What word means happiness and laughter in the poem?|mirth|Mirth.","t|The poem is about trains.|F|It is about families."])
E(7,"Unit 7: A Birthday Letter, The Stick-together Families, The Christmas Truce","christmas-truce","The Christmas Truce (Aaron Shepard)",
 "On a winter night in 1914, enemy soldiers on a battlefield put down their guns to sing.",
 "The text is a letter written on Christmas Day 1914 by a British soldier to his sister Janet. He tells her about Christmas Eve on the battlefields of France, when he sang carols with enemy soldiers. It seems almost like a fairy tale, and all enmity was forgotten as British and German soldiers celebrated Christmas together.",
 "The truce shows that ordinary people on both sides longed for peace and that kindness can cross the lines of war.",
 "Learn the words truce, dugout, trench, carol, enmity and replacements. Note the letter format: date, greeting My dear sister Janet and the closing.",
 ["truce","trench","carol","enmity","letter"],
 ("The soldiers fought hard on Christmas Eve.",["They sang carols together.","Enmity was forgotten."]),
 ["m|Who wrote the letter?|A British soldier|A German general|A king|A British soldier to his sister.","m|When was the truce?|Christmas 1914|Easter 1950|Diwali 1947|1914.","t|Enemy soldiers sang carols together.|T|Yes.","m|What does 'truce' mean?|A pause in fighting|A war|A feast|A truce stops fighting.","s|To whom was the letter written?|Janet;sister;his sister|His sister Janet.","t|The truce happened in India.|F|It happened in France."])
